# 7-Segment LED Display Projects

This repository contains a collection of beginner-friendly 7-segment LED display projects created to explore and understand the fundamentals of controlling a 7-segment display with an ESP32.

Each project focuses on a different technique for displaying numbers, controlling individual segments, creating counters, handling buttons, and managing timed displays. Together, they provide a structured introduction to 7-segment displays before moving on to more advanced display and embedded systems projects.

These examples demonstrate concepts such as segment control, digit patterns, counting, timing, button input, serial input, and reusable display functions. They can also serve as reference implementations that can be reused and expanded in future robotics, IoT, and embedded systems projects.

## Projects Included

### Number Counter

Displays a continuously increasing number on the 7-segment display.

### Button Counter

Uses a push button to increase the displayed number whenever the button is pressed.

### Countdown

Counts down from a starting number to zero.

### Segment Test

Activates each segment individually to verify that the display is wired and functioning correctly.

### Custom Digit Patterns

Creates custom segment patterns to control how numbers or symbols appear on the display.

### Adjustable Counter

Allows the counting behavior to be adjusted through user input.

## Learning Objectives

By completing these projects, you will gain experience with:

* Controlling individual 7-segment display segments
* Creating and using digit patterns
* Displaying numbers from 0–9
* Testing individual segments
* Using variables for counting and display control
* Creating counters and countdowns
* Using timing functions
* Reading push-button input
* Processing serial input
* Creating custom display patterns
* Building reusable display functions

These projects provide a solid foundation for understanding 7-segment LED displays and demonstrate how they can be used for counters, timers, indicators, and other embedded systems applications.
